SOAPBOX: I put in a fairly serious effort on this one though I had an
SOAPBOX: obligation that took me out for quite a bit of prime 40-meter
SOAPBOX: time Friday evening. My goal was to do better than last year
SOAPBOX: which I accomplished. Going up against high power stations that
SOAPBOX: have directional and/or other superior antennas is challenging
SOAPBOX: for sure, but I am content with my station right now. Last year I
SOAPBOX: had 279 contacts and this year I had 300. Last year my score was
SOAPBOX: 20,925. This year I understood the scoring better and worked hard
SOAPBOX: on getting Multipliers and longer distance contacts, so my score
SOAPBOX: was well over double at 56,547. I definitely enjoy FT4 more than
SOAPBOX: FT8. I don?t understand why more people don?t work FT4 since it?s
SOAPBOX: twice as fast. There were loads of people operating not in the
SOAPBOX: contest so quite a few contacts got stuck in a seemingly endless
SOAPBOX: loop. I discovered that if I forced my system to send the RR73
SOAPBOX: after the necessary contest exchange was completed, it would
SOAPBOX: force his system to send the 73 and end the contact with
SOAPBOX: everybody?s computer happy. I knew about the ?Right Click H?
SOAPBOX: toggle feature in WSJT-X 2.7.0 RC6, but I don?t have RC6 loaded.
SOAPBOX: Had I known there would be so many on frequency, not in the
SOAPBOX: contest, I would have loaded it. I was fortunate to work many
SOAPBOX: PVRCers including the following Central Virginia PVRC members:
SOAPBOX: NN4RB, K4XL, and W1IE on 3 bands. Thanks for the Qs!
